# Turf Community Edition License

Version 1.0 · Last updated 2026-11-23 · Permanent URL https://turf.build/license/v1/

Copyright Turf LLC (https://turf.build). All rights reserved.

Turf is proprietary software. To accept the Turf license terms (version 1.0,
https://turf.build/license/v1/), run `turf license accept` or set TURF_ACCEPT_EULA=yes.
Free for individuals, education and small businesses within the Community limits.
Government and larger organizations need a commercial license.

**If you have a commercial agreement.** If you or your organization has a signed Order Form
or Pilot Agreement with Turf LLC for the Software, or an order for the Software placed
through a reseller that Turf LLC has authorized, and it is still in effect, that agreement
governs your use. These Community Terms do not apply to you.

**Parties.** This Agreement is between Turf LLC, an Oregon limited liability company
("Provider") and the person or organization that accepts it ("Customer"). If you accept for
an organization, you confirm that you are authorized to bind it, and "Customer" means that
organization.

**How Customer accepts.** Customer accepts this Agreement by running `turf license accept`,
by answering yes when the Software asks, or by setting TURF_ACCEPT_EULA=yes or an equivalent
flag or configuration value, or by having a script, tool or AI agent do any of these on its
behalf. No signature is needed.

**What Customer confirms.** By accepting, Customer represents that its use is a Permitted
Use, and that whoever gave the acceptance had authority to do so. Customer repeats the first
statement each time it uses the Software under these Community Terms.

## Order Form

**Framework Terms.** These Community Terms incorporate the Common Paper Software License
Standard Terms Version 1.1, posted at
https://commonpaper.com/standards/software-license-agreement/1.1, by reference. A copy is in
the STANDARD-TERMS.md file distributed with the Software. Those Standard Terms and the Key
Terms below are the Framework Terms. Where this page and the Standard Terms differ, this page
controls. Capitalized words have the meanings given on this page or in the Standard Terms.

**Software.** Turf Community Edition: Turf as Provider distributes it in executable form,
namely the `turf`, `turf-mcp-server`, `turf-engine`, `turf-builtin-provider` and `turf-ui`
programs, the container images that hold them, and any Updates Provider makes available.

**Order Date.** The Effective Date.

**Subscription Period.** Month to month from the Effective Date, renewing automatically until
this Agreement ends as described under Changes to the Standard Terms.

**Fees.** None.

**Permitted Uses.** Customer's own internal purposes, including building and operating
infrastructure and services that Customer provides to its own customers, and only:

1. while Customer is an Eligible User and stays within the Community Limits; or
2. for Evaluation.

**Eligible User.** Any of the following:

1. an individual using the Software for personal, non-commercial purposes;
2. an accredited educational institution, and its students and staff, using the Software for
   teaching or non-commercial research;
3. a non-commercial open source project;
4. a Small Business.

**Small Business.** An organization that, together with its Affiliates, has fewer than 100
employees and contractors and had less than US$10,000,000 in revenue in its last fiscal year.

**Government.** A Government Entity is not an Eligible User at any size, except as an
educational institution above. A Government Entity is a national, regional or local government
body or agency, or an organization that a government owns or controls.

**Community Limits.** Counting all use of the Software by Customer and its Affiliates:

1. no more than 10 Billable Workspaces; and
2. no more than 32 GPUs in the infrastructure the Software manages, whether in production or
   not.

A Production Workspace is a workspace that manages production infrastructure. Workspaces used
only for development, staging, testing or previewing a change are not Production Workspaces
and are not counted. Billable Workspaces is the greater of (a) the number of Production
Workspaces and (b) the total number of resources that Production Workspaces manage, divided by
1,000 and rounded up. Ten Billable Workspaces therefore allows up to 10 Production Workspaces
managing up to 10,000 resources between them, at any size each.

**Evaluation.** Any organization, including a Government Entity, may use the Software for
non-production evaluation for 30 days from its first use.

**Grace period.** If Customer stops being an Eligible User, or exceeds the Community Limits,
it may keep using the Software for 90 days while it arranges a commercial license.

**Use for others.** Running the Software as a contractor, integrator or agent for an
organization that is not an Eligible User, to manage that organization's infrastructure, is
not a Permitted Use. Providing Customer's own infrastructure or services to such an
organization as Customer's customer is permitted.

**License Limits.** Customer will not (a) offer the Software itself to others as a hosted or
managed service; or (b) embed or bundle the Software in a product or service supplied to
others. Commercial, OEM and redistribution licenses are available from Provider.

**Warranty Period.** None. Sections 5.2 to 5.4 of the Standard Terms do not apply. The
Software is provided as is.

**Backups.** Customer is responsible for backing up its infrastructure state, configurations
and data.

**Services.** None. No support or maintenance is included.

**Deletion Procedure.** Customer will uninstall and delete the Software and stop using it.

**Usage Data.** The Software reports usage data to Provider by default, as described at
https://turf.build/telemetry/. Customer may turn this off by setting TURF_TELEMETRY=off. The
Software records Customer's acceptance (the terms version, the time and the method) on
Customer's system, and reports the terms version and the method to Provider when usage
reporting is on.

